{% from "partials/reply.md.j2" import reply %}
{% macro findings(categories, examples, consequence, why="") %}
## Findings file

One file at the target path. Finding IDs are slugs, `<category>-<short-kebab-slug>`
— categories here: {{ categories }}. Examples: {{ examples }}. The category
names the lane; the slug says what the finding *is*, because IDs get quoted in
commit messages and conversation with no file open beside them.

Per finding:

- ID.
- Location — file and line, or a short quote of the thing.
- What is wrong.
{% if why %}
- Why — {{ why }}
{% endif %}
- **Consequence** — {{ consequence }} Required: a finding with no consequence
  is deweighted by the judge and may be dropped.
- The specific fix, or a direction for one.

No severity tags; rank nothing. The judge does severity.

Report what you can show. An unconfirmed suspicion is investigated until it is
a finding or left out — maybes dilute the real findings.

Nothing to report → write a one-line file saying "No findings." and reply
`no-findings`. The token is not a count: it lets the orchestrator skip a
responder and judge with nothing to do, while the file remains in the audit
trail.
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro findings_reply(commit="the commit you reviewed", escalate="") %}
{{ reply(ok='`findings` when the file lists any, `no-findings` when it says "No findings." — one or the other, always',
         out="the notes file",
         escalate=escalate,
         commit=commit) }}
{%- endmacro %}
